WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.920
Hi, I'm Bill Badran.
I'm a financial planner.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:11.900
And to me, a financial plan should start
with something more important than numbers.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.670
Your life.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.270
The retirement you're looking forward to.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.480
The people you want to take care of.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.630
The freedom to do more
of what matters to you.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:32.060
For more than fifteen years, I've worked with individuals
and families to help them plan for goals like these.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.950
And I've noticed something.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:34.970
People work hard.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.450
They save.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.100
They try to make good decisions.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:44.180
But often, their financial lives
have grown one piece at a time.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.300
A retirement account here.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.730
An insurance policy there.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.340
A will that hasn't been reviewed in years.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.190
Each piece may make sense on its own.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.310
But who's looking at how
they all fit together?

00:01:00.841 --> 00:01:04.741
That's why I created The
Guide to Financial Prosperity.

00:01:04.791 --> 00:01:10.591
To help you see the bigger picture
— and take a clearer next step.

00:01:10.771 --> 00:01:15.101
Inside, we explore five
common blind spots.

00:01:15.151 --> 00:01:19.741
Finances that are scattered,
without one coordinated plan.

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:24.001
Focusing on returns without
understanding fees and taxes.

00:01:24.051 --> 00:01:28.211
Letting fear drive investment decisions.

00:01:28.261 --> 00:01:32.281
Guessing what retirement
will actually cost.

00:01:32.331 --> 00:01:39.811
And asking for advice after a major decision,
when some options may already be gone.

00:01:40.098 --> 00:01:45.608
They're easy mistakes to make, especially
when nobody has shown you what to look for.

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:47.838
So I've kept this guide simple.

00:01:47.888 --> 00:01:49.298
Everyday examples.

00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:51.328
Practical questions.

00:01:51.378 --> 00:01:53.508
No complicated jargon.

00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:58.443
And at the end, five questions to
help you look at your own situation.

00:01:58.623 --> 00:02:00.883
You don't need perfect answers.

00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:04.963
You just need an honest starting point.

00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:08.893
That's how I approach
financial planning, too.

00:02:08.943 --> 00:02:11.583
We start with you:

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:13.973
your family,

00:02:14.023 --> 00:02:14.773
your priorities,

00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:16.283
your goals.

00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:19.203
Then we look at the whole picture —

00:02:19.253 --> 00:02:21.323
your investments,

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:22.263
taxes,

00:02:22.313 --> 00:02:23.223
insurance,

00:02:23.273 --> 00:02:23.843
retirement,

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:25.463
and estate planning —

00:02:25.513 --> 00:02:29.123
and build one coordinated plan.

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:33.973
A plan we revisit as your life changes.

00:02:34.257 --> 00:02:41.267
My role is to help you understand your choices
and how they connect to the future you want.

00:02:41.487 --> 00:02:43.657
Take a few minutes to read the guide.

00:02:43.707 --> 00:02:47.837
And when you're ready, let's
talk about what matters to you.

00:02:48.107 --> 00:02:50.537
You've worked hard for what you have.

00:02:50.897 --> 00:02:53.867
Let's build a plan for what comes next.
